   <notesStmt>
    <note type="project" target="#dat1">This draft probably dates to early 1888, not long before the poem appeared in the <hi rend="italic">Herald</hi>. Whitman apparently expected to see it in the June issue of <hi rend="italic">Lippincott's,</hi> and when the magazine appeared on May 19 without mention of it, Whitman "wrote withdrawing it, at the same time sending a copy to The Herald," so that it could be published before his birthday, May 31. (See <hi rend="italic">With Walt Whitman in Camden</hi> [Boston: Small, Maynard &amp; Company, 1906] <ref target="med.00001.052">1:179&#8211;180</ref>).</note>

     <note type="work_relations" cert="high">"A Carol Closing Sixty-Nine" was first published in the 21 May 1888 issue of the <hi rend="italic">New York Herald</hi>. It was reprinted in <hi rend="italic">November Boughs</hi> later that year.</note>
